# daemon on -- see usr2-state-machine's env for the identical contract this
# scenario borrows: each of the three phases in driver.sh stops the current
# generation and calls prober_boot again for a fresh one, and the daemon-off
# alternative tracks the master by $! (the launcher's own pid) -- reaped the
# instant the master forks away, and useless for a driver that needs to
# re-target THIS generation's own master a second later (QUIT/TERM) or
# distinguish "the master we just booted" from "nothing ever booted"
# (SIGKILL). PROBER_DAEMON_MODE=on's pidfile-based tracking, which this
# driver re-exports before calling prober_boot itself, is what makes a
# second, third and fourth boot inside one driver process reliable.
PROBER_DAEMON_MODE=on

# Phase 3 (the non-vacuity SIGKILL control) deliberately kills a worker, so
# the master legitimately logs [alert] "worker process <pid> exited on
# signal 9" -- exactly worker-death's own PROBER_ALLOW_LOG, exempting the
# same line for the same reason: this is the fault the scenario provokes on
# purpose, not a server-side failure prober_scrape_log should red on. Pinned
# to "exited on signal 9" specifically, never a blanket allow -- a FAULT
# signal (SIGSEGV=11, SIGABRT=6, SIGBUS=7) carries an [alert] this pattern
# does not match and stays fatal.
PROBER_ALLOW_LOG='worker process [0-9]+ exited on signal 9'
